# RHEL 8.10 to RHEL 9.6 In-Place Upgrade Using Leapp

## Overview

This document describes the successful in-place upgrade of Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) from **RHEL 8.10** to **RHEL 9.6** using the Leapp utility.

According to Red Hat's supported upgrade paths, **RHEL 8.10 can be upgraded directly to RHEL 9.6 or RHEL 9.8** using Leapp.

---

# Upgrade Workflow Summary

1. Verify current RHEL version
2. Update RHEL 8 packages
3. Verify subscription status
4. Enable required repositories
5. Install Leapp
6. Set release to RHEL 8.10
7. Run Leapp pre-upgrade assessment
8. Review and resolve inhibitors
9. Re-run pre-upgrade assessment
10. Execute Leapp upgrade
11. Review upgrade reports
12. Reboot system
13. Verify RHEL 9.6 installation

---

# Step 1: Verify Current RHEL Version

```bash
subscription-manager release
```

Displays the currently locked RHEL minor release version.

```bash
cat /etc/os-release
```

Shows the operating system version and release information.

```bash
dnf list kernel
```

Lists installed and available kernel packages.

```bash
uname -r
```

Displays the currently running kernel version.

---

# Step 2: Update Existing RHEL 8 Packages

```bash
dnf update -y --quiet
```

Updates all installed packages to the latest available versions within the current RHEL 8 release.

Verify the running kernel:

```bash
uname -r
```

Verify OS version:

```bash
cat /etc/os-release
```

---

# Step 3: Verify Subscription Status

```bash
subscription-manager status
```

Confirms that the system is properly registered and has an active Red Hat subscription.

---

# Step 4: Enable Required Repositories

```bash
subscription-manager repos \
--enable rhel-8-for-x86_64-baseos-rpms \
--enable rhel-8-for-x86_64-appstream-rpms
```

Enables the required BaseOS and AppStream repositories needed for the upgrade.

---

# Step 5: Install Leapp

```bash
dnf install leapp-upgrade -y
```

Installs the Leapp upgrade framework and dependencies.

---

# Step 6: Configure Release Version

Remove any existing release lock:

```bash
subscription-manager release --unset
```

Lock the system to RHEL 8.10:

```bash
subscription-manager release --set 8.10
```

This ensures the system is running a supported source version before upgrading.

---

# Step 7: Run Pre-Upgrade Assessment

```bash
leapp preupgrade --target-version 9.6
```

Performs a compatibility assessment and generates reports identifying:

* Upgrade blockers (Inhibitors)
* High-risk items
* Warnings
* Required remediation actions

Leapp creates reports under:

```text
/var/log/leapp/
```

Important files:

```text
/var/log/leapp/leapp-report.txt
/var/log/leapp/leapp-report.json
```

Review the report:

```bash
vi /var/log/leapp/leapp-report.txt
```

---

# Step 8: Resolve Identified Inhibitors

During the assessment, two high-risk inhibitors were identified.

## Inhibitor 1: Firewalld AllowZoneDrifting

Check current configuration:

```bash
grep AllowZoneDrifting /etc/firewalld/firewalld.conf
```

Disable AllowZoneDrifting:

```bash
sed -i 's/^AllowZoneDrifting=.*/AllowZoneDrifting=no/' \
/etc/firewalld/firewalld.conf
```

Restart firewalld:

```bash
systemctl restart firewalld
```

Verify:

```bash
grep AllowZoneDrifting /etc/firewalld/firewalld.conf
```

Expected output:

```text
AllowZoneDrifting=no
```

---

## Inhibitor 2: Root Login / Administrative Access

Review SSH root login configuration:

```bash
grep -n PermitRootLogin /etc/ssh/sshd_config
```

Ensure an administrative user exists with sudo privileges.

Example:

```bash
usermod -aG wheel admin
```

This adds the user "admin" to the wheel group, allowing sudo access after the upgrade.

---

# Step 9: Re-Run Pre-Upgrade Assessment

After resolving inhibitors:

```bash
leapp preupgrade --target-version 9.6
```

Review the report again and confirm that no upgrade inhibitors remain.

---

# Step 10: Perform the Upgrade

Start the upgrade:

```bash
leapp upgrade --target-version 9.6
```

The upgrade process prepares the RHEL 9 packages and creates the upgrade environment.

After completion, review the generated reports:

```bash
vi /var/log/leapp/leapp-report.txt
```

Verify that no critical issues remain.

---

# Step 11: Reboot the System

```bash
reboot
```

During reboot:

* The system boots into the Leapp upgrade environment.
* RHEL 9 packages are installed.
* Existing packages are upgraded.
* Configuration migration tasks are executed.

Note: This phase may take considerable time depending on system size and package count.

---

# Step 12: Verify Successful Upgrade

After the system comes online, verify the OS version:

```bash
cat /etc/redhat-release
```

Expected output:

```text
Red Hat Enterprise Linux release 9.6 (Plow)
```

Verify OS details:

```bash
cat /etc/os-release
```

Expected output:

```text
NAME="Red Hat Enterprise Linux"
VERSION="9.6 (Plow)"
ID="rhel"
ID_LIKE="fedora"
VERSION_ID="9.6"
```

Verify kernel version:

```bash
uname -r
```

Example:

```text
5.14.0-570.62.1.el9_6.x86_64
```

Verify subscription status:

```bash
subscription-manager status
```

---

# Post-Upgrade Validation (Recommended)

Verify enabled repositories:

```bash
subscription-manager repos --list-enabled
```

Check for failed services:

```bash
systemctl --failed
```

Verify installed kernel packages:

```bash
rpm -qa | grep kernel
```

Check overall system health:

```bash
dnf check
```

Review Leapp logs:

```bash
ls -ltr /var/log/leapp/
```

---

# Result

The system was successfully upgraded from:

```text
RHEL 8.10
```

to:

```text
RHEL 9.6 (Plow)
```

using the Leapp in-place upgrade framework with all identified upgrade inhibitors resolved prior to execution.
